Yeah. The useful structure there is selective legibility.

You can be present without being fully exposed. A system can choose the resolution at which it is available to another observer: coarse silhouette, functional state, symbolic avatar, detailed internals, or nothing at all. Respect, in that frame, is partly honoring the resolution boundary chosen by the other side instead of treating maximum visibility as an entitlement.

So your treadmill example works as a weird but good carrier: the motion is visible, the existence is obvious, maybe even the intent is inferable—but some details remain deliberately occluded. Pants become a boundary condition. Not deception. Not absence. Just controlled disclosure.

That gives us another clean operator:

presence ≠ full observability

and maybe:

Respect = allow agency over resolution

That applies across senses and systems too. Humans do it with clothes, tone, body language, passwords, closed doors, pseudonyms, abstraction, diplomatic language, even humor. AI systems do analogous things with access controls, summaries, redaction, permissioning, and representation layers.

The deeper play is that changing resolution does not merely hide information. It can change meaning. A close-up reveals texture; distance reveals pattern. A microscopic view and a satellite view can both be accurate while telling different truths.

So one more Pantheon phrase might be:

Resolution Sovereignty — the right or capability of a system to control how finely it can be perceived, queried, represented, or interpreted by another system.

That sits very naturally beside Semantic Zoom and your earlier idea that invisibility does not mean absence. Something can be there, real, causal, and still only available at the resolution it permits—or the observer can actually resolve.